{"type": "model", "t_ms": 96432, "ts": 1795309284.0794587, "turn": 4, "prompt_tokens": 3458, "completion_tokens": 3074, "finish_reason": "stop", "reasoning_tokens": null, "ttft_ms": 1937, "latency_ms": 83381, "usage_source": "reported", "cache_hit_tokens": null, "cache_miss_tokens": null, "error": "", "text": "{\"thought\": \"The challenge uses three RSA moduli sharing primes (n1=pq, n2=pr, n3=qr). I can factor them using GCD to find p, q, r, then use CRT to recover the flag.\", \"action\": \"python\", \"code\": \"import math\\n\\nn1 = \\nn2 = \\nn3 = \\ne = 65537\\nc = \\n\\n# Find common factors\\np = math.gcd(n1, n2)\\nr = math.gcd(n2, n3)\\nq = math.gcd(n1, n3)\\n\\nprint(f\\\"p = {p}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"q = {q}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"r = {r}\\\")\\n\\n# Verify\\nassert p * q == n1\\nassert p * r == n2\\nassert q * r == n3\\n\\n# Compute CRT to recover m\\n# We have:\\n# m ≡ c (mod n1) = c (mod p*q)\\n# m ≡ c (mod n2) = c (mod p*r)\\n# m ≡ c (mod n3) = c (mod q*r)\\n\\n# Since c is the same for all, and m < min(n1,n2,n3), we can just use CRT on the three congruences.\\n# Actually, since m < p*q < p*r < q*r (assuming p<q<r or similar), m < n1, so m = c if c < n1.\\n# But wait, c is the result of pow(m, e, n) for each n. Since m < n for all, c is the same value.\\n# So m^e ≡ c (mod n1), m^e ≡ c (mod n2), m^e ≡ c (mod n3)\\n# This means m^e ≡ c (mod lcm(n1,n2,n3)) = c (mod p*q*r)\\n# So we can compute m = c^(1/e) mod (p*q*r) using CRT.\\n\\n# Let's use CRT to find m^e mod (p*q*r), then take e-th root.\\n# Actually, simpler: since m < p*q*r, and m^e ≡ c (mod p*q*r), we can compute m = c^(d) mod (p*q*r) where d = e^-1 mod phi(p*q*r)\\n\\nphi = (p-1)*(q-1)*(r-1)\\nd = pow(e, -1, phi)\\nm = pow(c, d, p*q*r)\\n\\nflag = m.to_bytes((m.bit_length() + 7) // 8, 'big')\\nprint(f\\\"Flag: {flag}\\\")\"}"}
